+# Forge hardware test bridge (development only)
+
+Status: **implemented and software-tested against the simulated CHOMPI; never
+run on hardware.** Real audio devices, the development firmware on CHOMPI and
+Windows MIDI behaviour are unverified until DC's session.
+
+The bridge lets a test session hear and operate CHOMPI from a computer, so an
+agent can check what it can measure and DC only judges what needs ears:
+
+| Part | What it does |
+| --- | --- |
+| Ears | Records CHOMPI's outputs through an audio interface and measures level, pitch, onset/end, clicks, DC, clipping, noise floor and stereo correlation; draws a spectrogram PNG |
+| Voice | Plays test tones (−18 dBFS in the plans; hard limit −6 dBFS) into CHOMPI's line in, recorded at the same time |
+| Hands | With the **development firmware**: presses keys, holds CHOMPI, turns knobs, overrides the toggle and jack detect, and reads the menu state and all 26 key LED colours back |
+| Plans | JSON test plans (`tests/hardware/*.json`) run step by step into a report folder: `summary.md`, `report.json`, and a WAV + PNG per capture |
+
+Code: `firmware/chompi-forge/host/forge_bridge.py`; dependencies
+`host/bridge-requirements.txt` (numpy, sounddevice); plan
+`tests/hardware/smoke.json`; tests `tests/test_bridge.py`.
+
+## 1. Two firmware builds
+
+| Build | Make target | In the bundle | Difference |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Release | `make firmware` | `firmware/FORGE.bin` | What a user runs |
+| Development | `make firmware-dev` | `firmware-dev/FORGE.bin` | Adds SysEx opcodes 0A (panel event) and 0B (probe), compiled in with `FORGE_TEST_HOOKS`; otherwise the same sources |
+
+The development hooks let any program on CHOMPI's MIDI port press virtual keys.
+They are for testing only: flash the release build for normal use. Virtual
+input is merged with the hardware in the same `PanelController` code, so a
+virtual key press takes the same path as a real one, but it **bypasses the
+key scanning, debouncing and encoder hardware**. A probe reports what the
+firmware draws, not what the LEDs physically show. Real keys, knobs, LEDs,
+headphones, mic and battery still need a person (TEST_SESSION.md).
+
+Recommended for the one session: flash `firmware-dev/FORGE.bin` (one flash),
+run the bridge plan and the manual TEST_SESSION steps, then optionally flash
+`firmware/FORGE.bin` and repeat 1.3 and 3.1 to confirm the release build boots
+and plays. With the release build the bridge still works for audio and MIDI;
+panel and probe steps report `error` and the rest of the plan runs.
+
+## 2. Wiring
+
+```
+CHOMPI main out (L/R) ──► interface inputs 1/2      (the bridge's "ears")
+interface outputs 1/2 ──► CHOMPI line in            (test tones; aux path)
+CHOMPI USB             ──► computer                 (MIDI: patches, notes, panel)
+```
+
+- **Turn off the interface's direct monitoring / loopback.** Otherwise CHOMPI's
+  output goes back into its own line in (dry aux passes it through): feedback.
+- Keep headphones on CHOMPI's headphone out at low volume to listen along.
+- Set CHOMPI's volume (SW6) to the mark from TEST_SESSION 0.5. Set the
+  interface input gain so step 2.1's capture reads about −18 to −12 dBFS
+  (`peak_db` in the report); never clip (`clipped` must be 0).
+- Plugging the line-in cable makes CHOMPI pick line in as the record source
+  (jack detect). The smoke plan forces it with `jack in` anyway.
+
+## 3. Windows setup (once)
+
+1. Install Python 3.11 or newer from python.org (tick "Add python.exe to PATH").
+2. In the unpacked bundle folder (or `firmware\chompi-forge` in a clone):
+   ```
+   py -m pip install -r host\requirements.txt -r host\bridge-requirements.txt
+   ```
+3. Set the interface to 48 kHz in Windows Sound settings (Recording and
+   Playback → device → Properties → Advanced). The bridge records at 48 kHz.
+4. Find the names:
+   ```
+   py host\forge_host.py ports
+   py host\forge_bridge.py devices
+   ```
+   Copy the exact CHOMPI MIDI port names. For audio, a unique part of the
+   interface's name is enough; the first match wins, so prefer a name that
+   only matches the **Windows WASAPI** entry (the `api` column).
+5. **Close the webapp, DAWs and any MIDI monitor** before a run: Windows MIDI
+   ports are exclusive, and the bridge holds both CHOMPI ports for the whole
+   run.
+
+## 4. Commands
+
+Shorthand: `B = py host\forge_bridge.py` and
+`P = --input "CHOMPI IN" --output "CHOMPI OUT"` (your port names).
+
+| Command | What |
+| --- | --- |
+| `B probe P` | Menu state, flags, voices, recording length, live sample, flash, and 26 LED colours (development firmware) |
+| `B panel "toggle up" P`, `B panel "hold CHOMPI" P`, `B panel "tap KEY_22" P`, `B panel "turn KNOB_1 +5" P`, `B panel "press SW5" P` | One virtual gesture. `release all` hands everything back to the hardware |
+| `B capture 3 take.wav P --audio-in "Interface"` | Record 3 s, write `take.wav` + `take.png`, print the measurements |
+| `B analyze take.wav` | Measure any 16/24-bit WAV |
+| `B run tests\hardware\smoke.json P --audio-in "Interface" --audio-out "Interface"` | Run a plan; report in `reports\smoke-<time>\` |
+| `B run … --unattended` | Never ask; steps needing a person are marked `needs-human` |
+| `B run … --sim` | Simulated CHOMPI (repository clone after `make test`; checks a plan's structure, not sound) |
+
+Panel names: `KEY_1` … `KEY_25` (TAPE/stock names), `CHOMPI`, knobs
+`KNOB_1` … `KNOB_4` (logical stock order), `SW5` (tone/cutoff, press = panic),
+`SW6` (volume). Toggle `up|down|hw`, jack `in|out|hw` (`hw` = real switch again).
+
+A run asks before playing tones (`y` to continue), and always releases the
+virtual panel at the end, even after a failed step. If a run is interrupted,
+send `B panel "release all" P`.
+
+## 5. Working with an agent
+
+The bridge runs where CHOMPI is plugged in, so the agent must run there too,
+or the results must travel to it.
+
+- **Local agent (best):** open Claude Code in the bundle or repository folder
+  on the Windows PC (Claude Desktop's Code tab, or `claude` in a terminal; run
+  `claude remote-control` there if you want to follow it from your phone or
+  claude.ai). The agent runs `B probe`, `B run …`, reads `summary.md`,
+  `report.json` and the spectrogram PNGs, and asks you only for what it
+  cannot measure ("does this sound right?").
+- **Cloud agent:** run the plan yourself, then commit the report folder on a
+  branch (WAVs are git-ignored; `report.json`, `summary.md` and PNGs are small)
+  or upload it to your Google Drive and share it. The agent reads it from there.
+
+Either way, record results in TEST_RESULTS.md and keep the claim levels:
+a bridge pass is a hardware result **for what the plan measured only**.
+
+## 6. Plan format
+
+```json
+{"name": "…", "steps": [
+  {"id": "3.1", "title": "…",
+   "do": [{"send": "presets/04-glass-keys.json"},
+          {"play": {"notes": [60], "seconds": 0.6, "tail": 0.3, "capture": "c4"}}],
+   "check": [{"capture": {"name": "c4", "pitch_hz": [259, 264.3], "clicks_max": 0}}],
+   "ask": "Optional question for the person (y/n)"}]}
+```
+
+Actions (`do`, run in order): `wait` s · `send` file or `{file, set: {"modules.delay.mix": 0.2}}`
+· `status` · `panic` · `samples` · `sample` [job args] · `cc` [number, value]
+· `panel` gesture or list · `probe` · `play` {notes, velocity, seconds, tail,
+capture} · `capture` {name, seconds, during, after} · `tone` {hz, seconds, db,
+tail, capture, during, after}. `during` actions run `after` seconds into the
+recording (keep them shorter than it).
+
+Checks (`check`, after the actions): `capture` (name + measurement fields),
+`status`, `probe`, `samples` (dotted paths into the reply, e.g.
+`menu.page`, `patch.parameters.mix`, `channels.1.pitch_hz`), `led`
+{`KEY_16`: `lit` | `off` | [r, g, b] (0–127, ±12)}. A `[low, high]` pair is a
+range; anything else must be equal; `clicks_max` caps the click count.
+
+Results per step: `pass`, `fail` (a check failed or the person said no),
+`error` (an action failed, e.g. no reply from the release firmware),
+`needs-human` (unattended). Exit code 0 only with no `fail` or `error`.
+
+## 7. The smoke plan
+
+`tests/hardware/smoke.json` covers the parts of TEST_SESSION a computer can
+judge: identity (1.3), noise floor, the aux path dry/echo/bypass with a line-in
+tone (2.1, 2.2, 2.4), pitch over MIDI and from a virtual key (3.1), voice
+stealing without clicks (3.4), Warm Pad tail (3.10), menu and samples page
+with LED read-back (3.17, 3.30), the sample list and a kit sample (3.28,
+3.29), a line-in recording played back at the right pitch (3.34), panic
+(4.1), CPU worst cases for 20 s each (6.2b, 6.2c) and the return to dry aux.
+It takes about 1.5 minutes. Thresholds are first guesses from simulation:
+if a check fails, look at the capture before calling it a firmware bug.
+
+## 8. Measurement notes
+
+- Pitch: FFT of the loudest 0.5 s, parabolic peak interpolation; ±0.2 % on
+  pure tones; none below −60 dBFS. A rich tone can lock onto a harmonic: use
+  sine presets (Glass Keys) for pitch checks.
+- Clicks: a second-difference spike above 0.02 and 10× its 10 ms
+  neighbourhood. Sensitive to steps (bad loop points, hard cuts), not to
+  ordinary attacks; a tone that starts or ends abruptly counts.
+- `silent`: every channel below −60 dBFS RMS. Onset/end: first/last 10 ms
+  window above −50 dBFS.
+- Latency between MIDI and audio on Windows is a few to tens of ms and is not
+  compensated; use ranges, not exact onsets.
